N1  - <div data-schema-version="9"><p>BOINC criterion evidence - Section 2 "Definition of symmetries and computing experiments", printed p. 5, verbatim: "The authors have conducted a series of computational experiments within the volunteer computing project Gerasim@Home1 based on BOINC platform [16]. During the experiments, all possible canonical forms of the DLSs were constructed that are symmetric in one plane and, at the same time, include row-inverse DLSs. This has not allowed to increase the previously found orthogonality characteristic." (footnote 1 gives http://gerasim.boinc.ru; [16] is Anderson &amp; Fedak, CCGrid 2006). Section 3 "Conclusion", p. 6, verbatim: "The corresponding experiments are being performed within the framework of the volunteer computing project Gerasim@Home." Section 4 "Acknowledgements", pp. 6-7, verbatim: "The authors of the paper express gratitude to citerra [Russia Team] from the Internet-portal BOINC.ru for his help in implementing some of the algorithms and finding a number of combinatorial structures with unique characteristics." SCOPE CAVEAT - the headline enumeration was NOT BOINC-derived: Section 2 p. 6, verbatim: "in 19 minutes on a Core i7 4770 processor in one thread, the generator defined the number of normalized centrally-symmetric DLSs of order 9, amounting to 327 254 016"; the order-9 ODLS experiment does not name a platform. Inclusion rests on the directly BOINC-derived plane-symmetry experiment plus the indirect BOINC.ru contribution. Full text: https://jpit.az/uploads/article/en/2019_2/CENTRAL_SYMMETRY_PROPERTIES_FOR_DIAGONAL_LATIN_SQUARES.pdf (byte-identical to parked/gerasim_2026-11-08/gerasim_ris/central_symmetry_2019.pdf). &nbsp;| note: Author list corrected from the publisher PDF: six authors. Crossref deposits the six affiliations as additional authors with empty name parts. "Oleq" is the spelling printed in the journal.</p>
</div>
